---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../consts';

// NOTE FOR THE DEVELOPER (not shown on the page): these terms are written to be
// accurate and readable, but they are NOT legal advice and I am not a lawyer.
// Before you rely on them for the App Store, for paid subscriptions, or for
// EU/UK traffic, have a real lawyer read them. See COMPLIANCE.md.
//
// Keep this in sync with what Questling ACTUALLY is:
//   - free, no payments of any kind yet (Quest Plus is not live: FREE_COUNT=999)
//   - no account required to play; an optional account saves progress
//   - the PARENT operates the app; the child never uses a device
//   - photos stay on the device and are never uploaded
// If any of those change, this page has to change with them.

const title = 'Terms of Use | Questling';
const description =
  'The rules for using Questling: who it is for, what it costs, safety and supervision, and what I do and do not promise. Written in plain language.';

const lastUpdated = 'September 23, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h1>Terms of Use</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {lastUpdated}</p>

      <p class="lead">
        These are the rules for using Questling. I have written them the way I would
        explain them out loud, because terms nobody reads protect nobody. If something
        here is unclear, email me and I will fix the wording.
      </p>

      <h2>Who Questling is for</h2>
      <p>
        Questling is a tool for <strong>parents and guardians</strong>. You pick a quest,
        your child goes and does it in the real world, and you mark it done together. The
        child does not need a device, an account, or a screen of their own. That is the
        entire point of the product.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because of that, <strong>the account holder must be an adult</strong>, or a young
        person old enough to agree to terms where they live, acting with their own
        parent's knowledge. If you are supervising a child's quests, you are responsible
        for deciding which quests are suitable for that child.
      </p>

      <h2>Safety comes before the quest</h2>
      <p>
        Quests happen outside, in kitchens, at playgrounds, near water and in the dark.
        Every quest has a <strong>Parent prep</strong> note, and it is there to be read
        before you hand the quest over. You know your child, your garden, your street and
        your weather. I do not.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>You decide what is safe.</strong> Read the quest first. Skip it, change
          it, or supervise it more closely if that is the right call. Nothing in the app
          is an instruction you have to follow.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adapt for age.</strong> Age ranges are a guide, not a rating. A cautious
          six-year-old and a reckless six-year-old need different answers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Supervise anything with real risk.</strong> Water, heights, tools, fire,
          roads, and anything the quest tells you to supervise.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Questling suggests activities. It does not assess your child, your location, or
        your conditions, and it cannot. That judgement stays with you.
      </p>

      <h2>What it costs</h2>
      <p>
        Right now, <strong>nothing</strong>. Every quest is free, there is no subscription,
        and the app takes no payment details. You may see "Quest Plus" mentioned in the
        app; it is not switched on and nothing is being charged for. If that ever changes,
        the price and the terms will be shown clearly before anyone is asked to pay, and
        this page will be updated first.
      </p>

      <h2>Accounts, gems and rewards</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>An account is optional.</strong> You can browse and do quests without
          one. An account saves your progress and unlocks the memory journal, the rewards
          shop and the weekly streak.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Gems are not money.</strong> They are a number in your own app. They
          cannot be bought, sold, cashed out or transferred, and they have no value outside
          your family's copy of Questling.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You set the rewards.</strong> The shop is a place for you to write down
          what gems are worth in your house. Honouring a reward is between you and your
          child; I am not part of that.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep your sign-in to yourself.</strong> You are responsible for what
          happens under your account.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your photos are yours</h2>
      <p>
        Photos you add to the memory journal <strong>stay on your device</strong>. They
        are not uploaded, not synced, and I never see them. That also means I cannot
        recover them if you clear the app or lose the phone, so treat them as photos on
        a phone, not as a backup. The <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> covers this
        in full.
      </p>

      <h2>The quest content</h2>
      <p>
        The quests, the writing on this site and the app itself are mine. You are very
        welcome to use them with your own family, print one out, or tell a friend. Please
        do not republish the catalogue as your own, scrape it, or sell it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some quests are made with partners and their artwork belongs to them. Where that
        is the case the quest page says so and credits them by name.
      </p>

      <h2>Please do not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>break into, overload, or try to damage the app, the site, or its database</li>
        <li>use someone else's account, or pretend to be someone else</li>
        <li>use Questling for anything illegal, or to harm a child</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If an account is being used that way I will close it.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do not promise</h2>
      <p>
        Questling is offered <strong>as it is</strong>. I am one developer. The app may
        have bugs, may go down, may lose a sync, and may change or disappear. I do not
        promise it will always be available or always work perfectly, and to the extent
        the law allows, I am not liable for what happens while you use it, including
        injuries that happen during a quest you chose to do.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is not me dodging. It is the honest position: I write the quests, you decide
        which ones happen and supervise them.
      </p>

      <h2>Stopping</h2>
      <p>
        You can stop using Questling whenever you like. If you want your account and its
        data removed, email me at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
        and I will delete it. I am building a delete button into the app so you will not
        have to ask.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
      <p>
        If I change anything meaningful here I will update the date at the top. Carrying
        on using Questling after a change means the new version applies.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. A real person
          reads it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .container--narrow { max-width: 720px; }
  h1 { margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .updated { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; margin-top: 2.2rem; }
  p, li { color: var(--ink); }
  ul { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  a { color: var(--primary); text-decoration: underline; }
  .callout {
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.6rem;
    background: var(--primary-tint);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .callout h2 { margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .callout p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>
